Z||000000||fiero56||09-21-2001||11:03 PM||||It has been time for new rear tires on the Formula for months. When I bought the car, it had Pirelli HPs all around. I really like them, they are cheap, quiet, and stick fairly well. I am almost through my second set, and have decided to see what else is available.<BR>I've got my eye on Yokohama H/4 tires at the moment. They sound comparable to my Pirellis, and my nieghbor who has a set on his SE-R says they are decent(not great, but decent). Anyone used these before? Any cautions about them? I will probably try my hand at auto-x events a few times, nothing serious, but wondering how these will hold up. Particularly side-wall stiffness, I run my Pirellis at 42psi(tire max is 44psi), just to keep them from wearing uneven, but these "max" at 35psi. Any thoughts there?||||reg||1
Z||000001||Fformula88||09-21-2001||11:30 PM||||I've traditionally had Dunlop D60 A2's on my Fiero and loved them!  So when I needed new rears this fall I was very disappointed to find out they had been discontinued... BUT the replacement is the SP Sport A2, which is a much better tire!  I have some good d60 A2's on the front, which loose grip long before the new rear SP Sport A2's!  I love them!<P>I have also heard a lot of people who like the BF Goodirch tires for the Fiero.  <P>I know a lot of Probe owners on their forum like Yoko tires (my daily driver is a Probe).  I dunno if its the same model or not, but they are well respected.<P>------------------<BR>John  <BR>AOLIM: Fformula88<BR>    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Fiero2m6siga.JPG">     <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/halfpole.gif"> <P>1988 Fiero Formula<BR>1985 Fiero SE 2M6||||reg||1
Z||000002||Standard||09-22-2001||05:16 PM||||I just got a pair of Yoko Avid H4's on the front of my quad.  Don't have any complaints.  They stick good, really work good in wet weather.  And they look cool, too  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>------------------<BR>     <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/sig_pic_full.jpg">         <BR>1988 Quad 4 Coupe 5 speed<BR><FONT SIZE="1">1986 GT 5 speed, 1984 SC Auto<BR><FONT COLOR="red">AOL: LastMinuteBastrd  AIM: SavedbyJebus</FONT></FONT>||||reg||1
Z||000003||Monkeyman||09-22-2001||10:08 PM||||I running some Yokohama Avid T4s (all weather performance?) on 4Play.  I love 'em.  I don't know if they'd be sticky enough for autox, but they're great daily driver/cornering tires.  Fairly cheap, too.  I think I paid around $75/each for them, mounted and balanced.||||reg||1
Z||000004||HummelHund||09-22-2001||10:30 PM||||I too have Avid T4's (215/60TR14) on my '84 SE. I am very pleased by the performance of the tires, they're very sticky and yes, they do look very cool. They're obviously not the ultimate performance tire, but I think they'd be good for learning auto-x on, and you can drive them to the track without having to worry about wear. I got mine from the Tire Rack for around $50 a tire.<BR><P>------------------<BR>Sean W.<BR>'84 SE, The "Buzz Bomb". (restoration project/daily driver)<BR>'84 SE (Dad's Project)||||reg||1
Z||000005||fiero56||09-22-2001||10:57 PM||||I was looking at the T-4s until I noticed Yokohama gave the T-4 a higher tread-wear rating number and temp is rated "B". I don't really care about tire life too much(I'm lucky to be able to squeeze 25K out of my Pirellis before the cordes show through), but the H-4 is rated a little lower on t/w rating, and is AA like my Pirellis.<P>My main concern is sidewall stiffness. I prefer something stiff, rather than a tire that likes to "wallow" as I steer. The pirellis seem to have a great stiff side-wall. Tire-rack has 'em cheaper than my Pirellis too, but not by much.<P>Do you re-call what shipping was from them?||||reg||1
Z||000006||Standard||09-22-2001||11:26 PM||||My H4's were 54 bucks each, and i think shipping was like 16 bucks.  Total for a pair came to less than 130, around $125 i think.||||reg||1
